# Supply-chain gate. Refuse any package version published more recently than this,
# because a compromised maintainer account shows up as a brand-new release and the
# window in which nobody has looked at it yet is the whole of the exposure.
#
# The unit is DAYS, as a plain integer. Other package managers in this family take
# minutes or seconds, which is exactly how the wrong number gets written here - but a
# duration string like `2d` is at least loud: measured on npm 11.12.1, 11.16.0 and
# 12.0.2, it stops the install with `Invalid time value` rather than proceeding ungated.
#
# The dangerous value is the one npm accepts and nobody meant. `0` puts the cutoff at
# this instant, `-1` puts it tomorrow, and neither warns about anything - so the number
# matters more than the syntax does.
#
# Do not verify this with `npm config get before`. It answered a date on npm 11.12.1 and
# answers `null` on 11.16.0 and 12.0.2 while the gate is enforced exactly the same, so
# reading it proves nothing about a modern npm and once turned a green check red on a
# repository that was never ungated. `npm config get min-release-age` is worse: it reads
# back null whether the file took effect or not. Run `node tools/release-gate-check.mjs`,
# which asks npm to resolve a package and reads the cutoff out of the refusal.
min-release-age=2
